/**
 * South Dakota Secretary of State, June 2, 2026 primary.
 *
 * Results: the State Canvassing Board's official canvass with its
 * certificate, a scanned PDF with an unusable OCR layer. The build reads a
 * reviewed transcription (page, office, district, party code, candidate as
 * printed, statewide total). Its figures were taken from the Secretary of
 * State's results site (electionresults.sd.gov, election 773) and every
 * candidate total (149 rows in 53 contests) was compared by eye with the
 * "Total" row of the scan at 130 dpi; all matched, as did the names. The
 * canvass prints only contested primaries: U.S. Senate, U.S. House and
 * governor (Republican), 23 Senate and 27 House contests. House districts
 * elect two members (vote for two) except subdistricts 26A, 26B, 28A and 28B.
 *
 * Unopposed candidates do not appear on the primary ballot. Their nominations
 * are read from the Secretary of State's official primary candidate list
 * (vip.sdsos.gov, election 773), retained as the list's own CSV export, which
 * the page serves only in answer to its ASP.NET postback (the body below
 * carries the page's view state). Names annotated "(Withdrawn ...)" or
 * "(Successful Challenge - Decertified ...)" are dropped. A party with no
 * canvass contest in a district and exactly one remaining candidate is
 * reported as an unopposed nomination. Where two candidates filed for a
 * two-seat House district and so had no primary, both were nominated, but
 * the contract allows one uncounted nominee per contest, so those districts
 * are left out and named by the check. The list also carries mailing
 * addresses, which the adapter does not read.
 *
 * The governor's primary went to a July 28 runoff (no candidate reached 35
 * percent). The certified runoff canvass is a 162 MB scan, over the 25 MB
 * retention limit, and the results site's runoff page triples its precinct
 * and registration counts, so the runoff is not retained.
 */
